from pathlib import Path
r=Path.cwd();p=r/'tools/build-guides.py';s=p.read_text(encoding='utf-8-sig')
user=[
'Open your captured project or cache, then choose Database schema in the upper-right page selector. Select key in the kind selector and declared key in the state selector. A primary key declares how to identify a row, a unique key declares a distinct combination of column values, and a foreign key declares a reference to another table. These are captured declarations; DPH does not connect to your server or establish that the rules are deployed.',
'Select a key row. The memo begins with Key type, Ordered local columns, Referenced table, Ordered referenced columns and Relationships. Composite columns retain their written order. Named constraints retain their name; anonymous constraints use a source-local label, not a fabricated server name. Inline PRIMARY KEY, UNIQUE and REFERENCES declarations become single-column key records. Supported table constraints capture composite lists and ON DELETE or ON UPDATE actions. A primary key does not rewrite the separately captured explicit nullability of a column.',
'Scroll the command strip sideways to reach the relationship selector and Follow relationship. Choose owning table, local column, referenced table or referenced column, then click Follow relationship. DPH clears the filters and opens that exact captured row, including rows on later pages. Only uniquely matched evidence appears as a navigation choice. Missing or ambiguous declarations remain explained in the memo. An unqualified reference is not matched to a guessed schema; quoted names and case retain their exact captured spelling.',
'Relationship gaps include a missing column or table, duplicate declarations, omitted referenced columns, differing local and referenced column counts, duplicate constraint names and multiple primary keys in one declaration. DPH does not guess omitted primary-key targets or select an effective migration. Captured links complete means every requested evidence link was identified; it does not validate target uniqueness, data compatibility or database enforcement. Unsupported key expressions and tails remain explicit gaps. Inline reference actions beyond the supported column reader remain column gaps.',
'Filter by constraint name, table, source, key type such as foreign, referenced table or relationship status. First page and Next page show 250 matches. Open source requires the attached unchanged source and matching generation. Read docs/guides/Database_Keys_Run_and_Test.txt for explicit launch, practice and validation steps. The controlled fixture includes Child_FK referencing Parent, along with deliberately missing and ambiguous examples.'
]
engineering=[
'TDPHDatabaseSchema.Inspect emits key rows from supported captured CREATE TABLE entries. Each row preserves key_kind, constraint_name, ordered members, reference_table, ordered reference_columns, referential_actions, table, declaration_id and sanitized definition. Existing file, SHA256, generation and entry-start line provenance remain attached. Inline clauses are collected during column parsing and promoted only after the complete column entry passes its supported grammar, preventing an unsupported later clause from leaking a partial key.',
'Table constraints accept an optional CONSTRAINT name and PRIMARY KEY, UNIQUE or FOREIGN KEY with nonempty identifier lists. Foreign keys accept a qualified referenced table, optional referenced column list and bounded ON DELETE or ON UPDATE actions. Repeated members, expressions, malformed lists, unknown actions and unsupported tails yield key gaps. CHECK remains captured constraint text. Inline reference actions outside the existing column subset remain column gaps. Token and row bounds remain unchanged. PostgreSQL constraint documentation supplies the reference syntax: https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/ddl-constraints.html. This source reader does not implement that complete dialect.',
'ResolveKeys builds exact table and declaration-owned column indexes with multiplicity counts. Each key receives ordered links containing role, reference, position, state and target_id only for unique captured matches. The owning table link uses the exact parent ID. Referenced columns resolve only after a unique referenced table is found. Missing or ambiguous evidence never chooses a first or last declaration. Omitted referenced columns and arity mismatches receive explicit notes; multiple primary keys and duplicate names receive conflict flags. Relationship status describes evidence completeness only, not semantic validity, target eligibility or enforcement.',
'DPH.UI.Main.fmx serializes the key kind and declared key state plus a relationship selector and Follow relationship button in the horizontally scrolling schema toolbar. Main owns target ID strings separately from borrowed projection rows. Selection and capture replacement clear those strings. Follow resets filters, locates the exact target ID, computes its 250-row page and selects it. Existing source guards handle direct key navigation. No runtime controls, new storage migrations or SQL execution are introduced.',
'Run tools/build-database-keys.cmd elevated for captured resource and SQLite reopening tests, composite order, named and anonymous inline keys, self references, missing or ambiguous members and tables, omitted target columns, arity differences, repeated columns, duplicate primary keys and names, unsupported syntax, literal omission, guarded source reading and native filters and relationship navigation. The suite also retains prior schema and column checks. Run column and schema regressions and tools/build-wp0.cmd for the actual executable and FMX baseline. Practice data stays in fixed ignored bin/database-keys-validation.'
]
at=s.index('user_order = [')
addition="USER.append(("+repr('Inspect primary foreign and unique keys')+", "+repr(user)+"))\nENGINEERING.append(("+repr('Resolve captured key membership and references')+", "+repr(engineering)+"))\nfor chapters in (USER, ENGINEERING):\n for index, (heading, paragraphs) in enumerate(chapters):\n  chapters[index]=(heading,[p.replace('332 requirement records remain incomplete','331 requirement records remain incomplete') for p in paragraphs])\n\n"
s=s[:at]+addition+s[at:]
at=s.index('user_order = [');s=s[:at]+s[at:].replace("'Inspect column types defaults and constraints',", "'Inspect column types defaults and constraints', 'Inspect primary foreign and unique keys',",1)
s=s.replace("'guide_version': 19","'guide_version': 20")
# Earlier chapters describe the earlier text-only view; explicitly point to the new key chapter.
s=s.replace('Constraint text does not establish key membership, referenced-column relationships, valid expression semantics or deployed enforcement. Those are separate investigations.', 'Constraint text alone does not establish key membership. The key view described in Inspect primary foreign and unique keys now captures supported membership and reference evidence. Valid expression semantics and deployed enforcement require separate investigation.')
p.write_text(s,encoding='utf-8')
p=r/'README.md';s=p.read_text(encoding='utf-8-sig');s+='\nRequirement 05.05: Database schema now exposes captured primary, foreign and unique keys with ordered columns, exact declaration ownership, evidence relationships and Follow relationship navigation. Missing, ambiguous and unsupported evidence is explained. No SQL is executed. See docs/guides/Database_Keys_Run_and_Test.txt.\n';p.write_text(s,encoding='utf-8')
p=r/'help/index.html';s=p.read_text(encoding='utf-8-sig');addition='<section id="database-keys"><h2>Inspect primary, foreign and unique keys</h2><p>'+('</p><p>'.join(user))+'</p></section>\n';assert '</main>' in s;s=s.replace('</main>',addition+'</main>',1);p.write_text(s,encoding='utf-8')
